.TH swait "1" "Slurm Commands" "Slurm 26.11" "Slurm Commands"

.SH "NAME"
swait \- Wait for a job's steps to complete

.SH "SYNOPSIS"
\fBswait\fR [\fIOPTIONS\fR]
[[\fIjobid\fR[_\fItask\fR] | \fBs\fR<\fIsluid\fR>][.\fIstepid\fR]]

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
\fBswait\fR blocks until there are no running steps and no pending
\fB\-\-async\fR steps. Use \fBswait\fR in a batch script to wait for
all \fB\-\-async\fR steps to complete. With a
\fIjobid\fR.\fIstepid\fR target it instead waits for that one step.

A \fIjobid\fR.\fIstepid\fR target that is still queued is waited on only
when the step was submitted with \fB\-\-async\fR. A pending
\fB\-\-async\fR step holds the whole\-set wait open, so \fBswait\fR
blocks until that step runs and ends. A queued synchronous step does not,
so the set can drain before the step starts.

\fBswait\fR talks directly to the stepmgr; it does not poll
\fBslurmctld\fR. The target job must have stepmgr enabled (see
\fBREQUIREMENTS\fR).

The positional argument is a numeric job ID, a single array task
(e.g. \fI1000_3\fR), or a SLUID (e.g. \fIs4SNKN57XZTE00\fR). Task ranges,
task lists, and het\-job component offsets (\fIjobid\fR+\fIoffset\fR) are
not supported. When the target is an array job with more than one task,
a specific task must be selected with \fIjobid\fR_\fItask\fR. A
\&.\fIstepid\fR suffix may also be appended to any of these target
forms. The target must be a regular step, not a special step (batch,
extern, or interactive).

Heterogeneous (het) step \fIstepid\fRs are not supported; \fBswait\fR
exits 1, either when it parses the argument or when the stepmgr rejects
the subscribe request. See
<https://slurm.schedmd.com/heterogeneous_jobs.html>.

With no positional argument, \fBswait\fR consults \fBSLURM_JOB_SLUID\fR
first, and falls back to \fBSLURM_JOB_ID\fR. This lets \fBswait\fR run
inside a batch script or interactive allocation with no arguments and
automatically wait on the enclosing job's steps.

.SH "OPTIONS"
.TP
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print help information and exit.
.IP

.TP
\fB\-Q\fR, \fB\-\-quiet\fR
Suppress informational messages on stderr and step\-completion output on
stdout. This covers the whole\-set \fBsteps drained\fR line as well.
Errors will still be displayed. Mutually exclusive with
\fB\-\-verbose\fR.
.IP

.TP
\fB\-\-timeout\fR=\fISECS\fR
Give up after \fISECS\fR seconds and exit 2. The wait completes when the
job's steps drain, or when a \fIjobid\fR.\fIstepid\fR target ends.
\fISECS\fR must be a non\-negative integer. With no \fB\-\-timeout\fR,
or with \fB\-\-timeout\fR=0, \fBswait\fR blocks indefinitely.
.IP

.TP
\fB\-\-usage\fR
Print a short usage line and exit.
.IP

.TP
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-verbose\fR
Increase verbosity; may be specified multiple times for more detail.
Mutually exclusive with \fB\-\-quiet\fR.

At \fB\-v\fR, \fBswait\fR logs \fBsubscribed to stepmgr\fR
\fInode\fR on stderr once the stepmgr has accepted its subscribe
request. A script that must not signal a step before \fBswait\fR is
listening can wait for that line.
.IP

.TP
\fB\-V\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Print version information and exit.
.IP

.SH "OUTPUT"
With a \fIjobid\fR.\fIstepid\fR target, unless \fB\-\-quiet\fR is
given, each step completion is reported on stdout as one line:

.nf
StepId=\fIjobid\fR.\fIstepid\fR code=\fIexit\fR:\fIsig\fR [reason=\fISTATE\fR]
StepId=\fIjobid\fR.\fIstepid\fR never\-launched reason=\fISTATE\fR
.fi

\fBcode=\fR carries the step's exit status and the signal that killed
it, one of which is always 0. \fBreason=\fR is added only when the step
reached a failure state such as \fBTIMEOUT\fR or \fBNODE_FAIL\fR; a step
that ran to completion has none.

A step killed by a signal reports that signal in \fBcode=\fR. It
carries a \fBreason=\fR only when it also reached a failure state: a
step killed at its time limit is in \fBTIMEOUT\fR and reports
\fBreason=TIMEOUT\fR next to the signal, while a running step killed by
\fBscancel\fR(1) reports the signal alone. A nonzero \fIsig\fR, not the
presence of \fBreason=\fR, is therefore what identifies a signaled
step. Do not read the leading \fB0\fR in \fBcode=0:9\fR as success.

A step that never launched is reported as \fBnever\-launched\fR in place
of \fBcode=\fR. Such a step is always in a failure state, so it always
carries a \fBreason=\fR, normally \fBFAILED\fR or \fBCANCELLED\fR.

When the wait ends a final line is printed:

.nf
JobId=\fIjobid\fR steps drained
.fi

This line is printed whenever a wait actually completes. A
\fIjobid\fR.\fIstepid\fR wait that is satisfied by its target step
prints that step's line instead. \fB\-\-quiet\fR suppresses this line
too.

Nothing at all is printed when there is no wait to perform: when the job
already has no running steps and no pending \fB\-\-async\fR steps, or
when a \fIjobid\fR.\fIstepid\fR target has already ended. \fBswait\fR
returns immediately in both cases.

.SH "REQUIREMENTS"
The target job must have stepmgr enabled, either site\-wide via
\fBSlurmctldParameters=enable_stepmgr\fR in \fBslurm.conf\fR(5), or
per\-job via \fB\-\-stepmgr\fR to \fBsalloc\fR(1)/\fBsbatch\fR(1).
This can be verified with \fBscontrol show job <jobid>\fR; look for
\fBStepMgrEnabled=Yes\fR. Against a job without it, \fBswait\fR exits 1
with \fBdoes not have stepmgr enabled\fR rather than waiting.

A \fIjobid\fR.\fIstepid\fR target additionally requires the job's
stepmgr, which runs in the job's extern \fBslurmstepd\fR(8) on the job's
\fBBatchHost\fR (visible in \fBscontrol show job\fR), to be Slurm 26.11
or newer.
Against an older stepmgr the subscribe request fails and \fBswait\fR
exits 1; it does not fall back to a whole\-set wait. Note that
\fBslurmd\fR(8) may already be upgraded while a running job's
\fBslurmstepd\fR(8) is not, so the batch host's package version is not
on its own sufficient.

At most 64 \fBswait\fR processes may be subscribed to one job at a time.
Beyond that \fBswait\fR exits 1 with \fBsubscriber slots full\fR.

The target job must already be running. \fBswait\fR does not wait for a
job to start; against a \fBPENDING\fR job it exits 1 with
\fBis still pending\fR.

.SH "ENVIRONMENT VARIABLES"
.PP
Some \fBswait\fR options may be set via environment variables. These
environment variables, along with their corresponding options, are listed below.
(Note: Command line options will always override these settings.)

.TP
\fBSLURM_JOB_SLUID\fR
Used to identify the job to monitor when no positional argument is given.
Takes precedence over \fBSLURM_JOB_ID\fR.
.IP

.TP
\fBSLURM_JOB_ID\fR
Used to identify the job to monitor when no positional argument is given and
\fBSLURM_JOB_SLUID\fR is not set. Also used, together with
\fBSLURM_ARRAY_JOB_ID\fR and \fBSLURM_ARRAY_TASK_ID\fR, to confirm that
\fBSLURM_STEPMGR\fR describes the target array task.
.IP

.TP
\fBSLURM_STEPMGR\fR
Hostname of the job's stepmgr. When it is set and the target job matches the
job described by the environment, \fBswait\fR contacts that host directly and
never queries \fBslurmctld\fR. Otherwise \fBswait\fR looks the stepmgr up
through the controller.
.IP

.TP
\fBSLURM_ARRAY_JOB_ID\fR
Array master job ID of the enclosing job. Used together with
\fBSLURM_ARRAY_TASK_ID\fR to confirm that \fBSLURM_STEPMGR\fR describes the
target array task.
.IP

.TP
\fBSLURM_ARRAY_TASK_ID\fR
Array task offset of the enclosing job. See \fBSLURM_ARRAY_JOB_ID\fR.
.IP

.TP
\fBSLURM_CONF\fR
Location of the Slurm configuration file.
.IP

.SH "EXIT STATUS"
.TP
\fB0\fR
The wait completed: the step set drained, or the target step ended and
was reported. This reflects only that the wait completed, not a
\fIjobid\fR.\fIstepid\fR target's own result; see \fBcode=\fR in the
completion output for the step's actual outcome.
.IP

.TP
\fB1\fR
\fBswait\fR encountered an error.
.IP

.TP
\fB2\fR
\fB\-\-timeout\fR elapsed before the wait completed.
.IP

.SH "EXAMPLES"

Run multiple async steps from a batch script and block on them. The
\fB\-\-stepmgr\fR flag may be omitted if stepmgr is enabled site\-wide.

.nf
#!/bin/bash
#SBATCH \-n 16
#SBATCH \-\-stepmgr
srun \-\-async \-n 8 ./solve_a
srun \-\-async \-n 8 ./solve_b
swait
.fi

Wait on one step and read its exit code. \fBsrun\fR \fB\-\-async\fR
\fB\-\-parsable\fR prints the new step's \fIjobid\fR.\fIstepid\fR, which
is the target \fBswait\fR expects.

.nf
#!/bin/bash
#SBATCH \-n 16
#SBATCH \-\-stepmgr
step=$(srun \-\-async \-\-parsable \-n 8 ./solve_a)
srun \-\-async \-n 8 ./solve_b
swait "$step"   # prints solve_a's exit code if it is still running
swait           # then wait for everything else
.fi

If \fB./solve_a\fR ends before \fBswait\fR subscribes, nothing is
printed.

\fBswait\fR is only for \fB\-\-async\fR steps; if a script merely
backgrounds \fBsrun\fR commands with the shell, the shell's \fIwait\fR
builtin already covers that case and \fBswait\fR is not needed. The
exception is when a script mixes both: run \fBswait\fR followed by
\fIwait\fR so that the stepmgr's pending work and the shell's
background processes have both finished:

.nf
#!/bin/bash
#SBATCH \-n 16
#SBATCH \-\-stepmgr
srun \-n 8 ./solve_a &
srun \-\-async \-n 8 ./solve_b
swait
wait
.fi

